Computed tensor-layout v2 expected headers — `quant(topology)`, Go-produced.

These are NOT hand-written and MUST NOT be hand-edited. `quant(topology)` is
evaluated by the Go engine in ~/cozy/tensorfs and by nothing else: there is
deliberately no pyo3 binding for the evaluator, because v1 shipped three copies
of one matching rule and the copies were free to disagree about an admit. A
Python fixture generator here would be a fourth copy. Same posture tensorfs
itself takes at `python/tests/data/layout-*.json`.

Regenerate (from `~/cozy/tensorfs`, pgw#1621):

    go run ./scripts/compute_layout 'ltx2-upsampler.diffusers@1+plain.bf16@1' \
      > <pgw>/tests/testdata/layout_v2/'ltx2-upsampler.diffusers@1+plain.bf16@1.json'

    go run ./scripts/compute_layout 'flux2-klein.diffusers@1+cozy.nvfp4-flat@1' \
      | gzip -9 > <pgw>/tests/testdata/layout_v2/'flux2-klein.diffusers@1+cozy.nvfp4-flat@1.json.gz'

`go run ./scripts/compute_layout --list` prints every ratified pair with its
tensor count.

Why these two, and why not sdxl:

  ltx2-upsampler.diffusers@1+plain.bf16@1        9.8 KB, 1 component, 72 tensors
      The smallest pair in the corpus and the plain green/red case: one dtype
      (BF16), no optional keys, no reference tolerance. It is all conv/norm, so
      it is also the one pair a quant rule transforms nothing in.

  flux2-klein.diffusers@1+cozy.nvfp4-flat@1      5.6 KB gzipped (176 KB raw),
                                                 3 components, 1218 tensors
      The only SMALL pair that carries all three of the things the arm has to
      get right: multiple components with roles (text_encoder / denoiser / vae,
      so the manifest is prefixed), 200 `optional: true` keys (`input_scale`,
      `pre_quant_scale` — a calibration may not have produced them), and a
      REFERENCE-TOLERANT key, `vae.bn.num_batches_tracked`, whose dtypes are
      ["BF16", "I64"]. That last one is why acceptance is list membership and
      not equality: the reference packaging really did ship it as I64, and
      refusing it would refuse the checkpoint the topology was extracted from.

sdxl's headers are 300 KB and prove nothing these two do not.

Assertions live in tests/test_juggle_expected_header_pgw1621.py.
